    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Concrete Land"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Asmahan Bkerat’s feature directorial debut is an intimate, multi-generational portrait of a mixed-raced family fighting displacement and hostility in Jordan."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://d3cyv0xf0ss37v.cloudfront.net/2026/films/560x315/revolutionaries-never-die-01.jpeg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "BFI London Film Festival 2026"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Revolutionaries Never Die"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Framed as a cinematic letter to the late Lebanese filmmaker Jocelyne Saab, this evocative and timely work reanimates Saab’s 16mm archive as a living space of dialogue and political imagination."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://d3cyv0xf0ss37v.cloudfront.net/2026/films/560x315/once-upon-a-time-in-harlem-01.jpeg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "BFI London Film Festival 2026"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Once Upon a Time in Harlem"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Filmed by William Greaves in 1972 and completed by his son David 50 years later, this is a rapturous love letter to the Harlem Renaissance."
    }
  ]
